%N A048820 Binary expansion of the Minkowski-Bower constant.
%C A048820 Other fixed points (mod 1) are 0, 1/2 and 1-A048820. - Joseph Biberstine (jrbibers(AT)indiana.edu), Jun 10 2006
%C A048820 It is conjectured that Minkowski's question mark function has exactly five fixed points on [0,1], or four modulo 1; see Gayfulin and Shulga, Conjecture 1. - _Eric W. Weisstein_, Oct 08 2026
%D A048820 S. R. Finch, Mathematical Constants, Cambridge, 2003, pp. 441-443.
%H A048820 Steven R. Finch, <a href="http://www.people.fas.harvard.edu/~sfinch/constant/cntfrc/mnkwsk.html">Minkowski's Question Mark Function</a> [Broken link]
%H A048820 Steven R. Finch, <a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20010208143502/http://www.mathsoft.com/asolve/constant/cntfrc/mnkwsk.html">Minkowski's Question Mark Function</a> [From the Wayback machine]
%H A048820 D. Gayfulin and N. A. Shulga, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.10139">Diophantine properties of fixed points of Minkowski question mark function</a>, arXiv:1811.10139 [math.NT], 2018-2019; Acta Arithmetica 195 (2020), 367-382. See Conjecture 1.
%H A048820 <a href="/index/Me#MinkowskiQ">Index entries for sequences related to Minkowski's question mark function</a>
%e A048820 0.011010...
%t A048820 RealDigits[x /. FindRoot[MinkowskiQuestionMark[x] == x, {x, 42/100, 421/1000}, WorkingPrecision -> 100, MaxIterations -> 500], 2, 102, -1][[1]] (* _Eric W. Weisstein_, Oct 08 2026 *)
%Y A048820 Cf. A048817, A048818, A048819, A048821, A048822.
%Y A048820 Cf. A058914 (continued fraction of the Minkowski-Bower constant).
%Y A048820 Cf. A120221 (continued fraction of one minus the Minkowski-Bower constant).
